Ancillary tail slots
--------------------

lame_set_ancillary_slots() installs a plan of small data items that are
written into the physical tail of selected MP3 audio frames.  Each
lame_ancillary_slot names:

  - audio_frame: the zero based number of the encoded AUDIO frame that
    carries the data.  Numbering starts at 0 and counts only real audio
    frames, including the frames produced while flushing the encoder;
    ID3v2 and Xing/Info frames are not counted and do not consume a
    number.  Frame numbers in one plan must be strictly increasing.
  - data/size:  the 1..32 payload bytes.  The library makes its own
    copies, so the caller may reuse or free this memory as soon as
  lame_set_ancillary_slots() returns 0.

The plan must be installed before lame_init_params(); NULL with a count
of 0 clears the current plan.  Invalid arguments, calls after
lame_init_params() and allocation failures return -1 and leave any
previously installed plan untouched.  A non-empty plan is accepted only
for CBR encoding with equal input/output sample rates of 24, 44.1 or
48 kHz, mono/stereo/joint-stereo output and a bitrate of 64..160 kbps
that is legal for the configuration; lame_init_params() rejects anything
else.

For a frame of physical length L carrying a slot of n bytes, the bytes
at offsets L-n .. L-1 are exactly the slot data.  They are genuine
ancillary data: the quantizer is constrained before encoding so that no
scalefactor or Huffman bit (including data back-pointed from other
frames) can occupy them.  A slotted frame drains the bit reservoir, so
the next audio frame always has main_data_begin 0; the reservoir then
builds up and is borrowed again normally in the following slot-free
regions.  Header CRC, padding and Xing/Info tagging keep working, and an
encoder without a plan produces the identical bitstream as before.

Example:

    lame_ancillary_slot slots[2];
    unsigned char d0[4] = { 0xde, 0xad, 0xbe, 0xef };
    unsigned char d1[32];
    slots[0].audio_frame = 0;  slots[0].data = d0; slots[0].size = 4;
    slots[1].audio_frame = 7;  slots[1].data = d1; slots[1].size = 32;

    lame_t gfp = lame_init();
    /* ... configure a supported CBR mode ... */
    if (lame_set_ancillary_slots(gfp, slots, 2) != 0) { /* handle error */ }
    if (lame_init_params(gfp) != 0) { /* handle error */ }
    /* ... lame_encode_buffer() / lame_encode_flush() as usual ... */

Slots whose frame number is beyond the last frame actually emitted are
ignored and never cause extra audio frames to be generated.

